# Finnish → IPA transliteration rules (fi_ipa.rules)
#
# Source-Authors: Suomi, K., Toivanen, J. & Ylitalo, R.
# Source-Year: 2008
# Source-Title: Finnish sound structure: Phonetics, phonology, phonotactics and prosody
# Source-Container: Studia Humaniora Ouluensia 9. Oulu: Oulu University Press
# Source-Id: https://urn.fi/URN:ISBN:9789514289842
#
# Simplified: segmental phonemes only, no allophonic rules (like [h x ç] allophones,
# vowel centralisation, or sandhi / glottalisation). Quantity is handled purely
# by doubled letters → long segments (ː).
#
# Corroborated: Karlsson, F. (2018) Finnish: A Comprehensive Grammar,
# Routledge (ISBN 978-1-138-82103-3), ch. 2: letter table with ʋ and ɑ
# (p. 16); ng/nk as in the context rules below, rengas [reŋːɑs] and
# Helsinki [helsiŋki] (p. 14); doubled letters as length (p. 21). His h
# allophones [ɦ x ç] are the ones declared out of scope above. Fourteen
# printed words pinned in test_finnish_karlsson_words.py. JIPA has no
# Finnish Illustration (CrossRef record for ISSN 0025-1003, checked
# 2026-08).

# 0.  MACROS  (symbols in the source alphabet, case-sensitive)

$V = [ a ä e i o ö u y A Ä E I O Ö U Y ];          # orthographic vowels
$C = [ b c d f g h j k l m n p q r s t v w x z
       B C D F G H J K L M N P Q R S T V W X Z
       š Š ž Ž ' ];

# The same eight vowels on the output side. A left context is matched
# against text the transform has already rewritten, so a rule that looks
# backwards past the cursor has to name the IPA vowels, not the letters.
$Vout = [ ɑ æ e i o ø u y ];

# consonant letters & apostrophe


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 1.  CONTEXT-SENSITIVE RULES
#     Applied before the one-to-one grapheme → phoneme map.


# 1.1 THE VELAR NASAL
#     The source calls this "the only fully systematic exception" to the
#     one-grapheme-one-phoneme principle of Finnish orthography, because
#     the Latin alphabet has no letter for /ŋ/ and none was adopted (p. 141):
#
#       <n> before <k>          → ŋ    kenkä 'shoe'                  (p. 141)
#       <ng> between vowels     → ŋː   kengän 'of shoe', tango       (p. 141)
#       <ng> before a consonant → ŋ    Englanti [eŋlɑnt̪i]            (p. 57)
#       <gn>                    → ŋn   kognitio [koŋnit̪io]           (p. 57)
#
#     The length of intervocalic <ng> is not stipulated by the orthography
#     chapter; it follows from the phonotactics. A singleton /ŋ/ cannot
#     stand between vowels — "all consonants except /ŋ/ can occur" as a
#     word-internal C (p. 56) — and in native words /ŋ/ occurs only in
#     /ŋk/ (p. 57). The two environments the source transcribes, Englanti
#     with one and kengän with two, are exactly what that predicts.

[nN] } [kK] > ŋ ;

$Vout { [nN] [gG] } $V > ŋː ;
[nN] [gG] } $C > ŋ ;

[gG] } [nN] > ŋ ;


# 1.2 V / W → [ʋ]
#     The phoneme is the labiodental approximant, listed as /ʋ/ in group (1)
#     of the consonant inventory (p. 25). The graphemes <v> and <w> "are
#     non-distinct in Finnish in the sense that the choice between them
#     implies no difference in pronunciation"; <w> is not used for native
#     words in the standard orthography, only in trade names (pp. 142-143).

v  > ʋ ;
V  > ʋ ;
w  > ʋ ;
W  > ʋ ;

# NOTE: Because of the rules above, we do not use a separate fallback v/w rule
# below; v/w always go to ʋ.


# 1.3 POSTALVEOLAR FRICATIVE
#     The grapheme is <š>, and it is handled by the one-to-one map in
#     section 2.2 along with its geminate.
#
#     There is deliberately no <sh> digraph rule. The source licenses none:
#     /ʃ/ is a group (5) phoneme written <š> (pp. 25, 141). Meanwhile <sh>
#     arises constantly inside native compounds, because an obstruent may
#     precede /h/ across a morpheme boundary (p. 57, restriction 5) and
#     Finnish compounds put an -s against an h-initial second member —
#     keskus+hallinto, kuningas+huone, rakennus+hanke. A digraph rule
#     deletes the /s/ from every one of those.


# 1.4 GLOTTAL STOP
#     Apostrophe <'> marks a glottal stop in forms like <vaa'an>.

\' > ʔ ;


# 1.5 APPROXIMATE HANDLING OF FOREIGN LETTERS
#     These are crude defaults; edit as needed for the corpus.

c  > k ;
C  > k ;

q  > k ;
Q  > k ;

x  > k s ;
X  > k s ;

z  > s ;
Z  > s ;

å  > o ;
Å  > o ;


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# 2.  ONE-TO-ONE GRAPHEME → SEGMENT MAP
#     "A given vowel phoneme is always written with the same grapheme. This
#     is true of most consonant phonemes, too" (p. 20).
#
#     Long segments (double letters) are mapped to IPA Vː / Cː. The source
#     writes them instead as sequences of two identical phonemes — taakka
#     is /t̪ɑɑkkɑ/ there and tɑːkːɑ here (p. 19) — and marks the dental
#     place on /t̪ d̪/, which is left off here. Both are notational.
#
#     /dd bb gg ff ʃʃ/ occur only in recent loanwords and /hh/ only in
#     hihhuli, but they are mapped for completeness; /jj/ and /ʋʋ/ the
#     source reports do not occur at all (p. 56).


# 2.1 VOWELS

# Long vowels (double letters) - MUST come before short vowels

aa > ɑː ;
AA > ɑː ;

ää > æː ;
ÄÄ > æː ;

ee > eː ;
EE > eː ;

ii > iː ;
II > iː ;

oo > oː ;
OO > oː ;

öö > øː ;
ÖÖ > øː ;

uu > uː ;
UU > uː ;

yy > yː ;
YY > yː ;


# Short vowels

a  > ɑ ;
A  > ɑ ;

ä  > æ ;
Ä  > æ ;

e  > e ;
E  > e ;

i  > i ;
I  > i ;

o  > o ;
O  > o ;

ö  > ø ;
Ö  > ø ;

u  > u ;
U  > u ;

y  > y ;
Y  > y ;


# 2.2 CONSONANTS

# Long consonants (geminates) - MUST come before short consonants

pp  > pː ;
PP  > pː ;

tt  > tː ;
TT  > tː ;

kk  > kː ;
KK  > kː ;

bb  > bː ;
BB  > bː ;

dd  > dː ;
DD  > dː ;

gg  > ɡː ;
GG  > ɡː ;

mm  > mː ;
MM  > mː ;

nn  > nː ;
NN  > nː ;

ll  > lː ;
LL  > lː ;

rr  > rː ;
RR  > rː ;

ss  > sː ;
SS  > sː ;

ff  > fː ;
FF  > fː ;

hh  > hː ;
HH  > hː ;

šš  > ʃː ;
ŠŠ  > ʃː ;

jj  > jː ;


# Short consonants

# Core plosives
p   > p ;
P   > p ;

t   > t ;
T   > t ;

k   > k ;
K   > k ;

# Marginal voiced plosives (mainly in loans)
b   > b ;
B   > b ;

d   > d ;
D   > d ;

g   > ɡ ;
G   > ɡ ;

# Nasals
m   > m ;
M   > m ;

n   > n ;
N   > n ;

# Liquids
l   > l ;
L   > l ;

r   > r ;
R   > r ;

# Fricatives
s   > s ;
S   > s ;

f   > f ;
F   > f ;

h   > h ;
H   > h ;

# Postalveolar (from <š>, <sh> above)
# ʃ mapping already handled context-sensitively; duplicates here are harmless:
š   > ʃ ;
Š   > ʃ ;

# Approximants / glides
j   > j ;
J   > j ;

# /ʋ/ already handled as v/w in section 1.2.

:: NFC ;
